Falsifies `entropy_contract::verification::an_opcode_or_step_past_one_byte_is_refused`.

The defect: `request` lets an opcode of up to 0x1ff through. The opcode 0x100 is then built into a
word, and the shift drops its high bit, so the word carries opcode 0 and a client that asked for
one operation sends another. This is the release-build behavior the function had before it
refused out-of-range values in every build.

Expected to fail: the harness's `assert_eq!` that `request` is an error exactly when the opcode is
above 0xff, for an opcode from 0x100 to 0x1ff.
diff --git a/src/lib.rs b/src/lib.rs
--- a/src/lib.rs
+++ b/src/lib.rs
@@ -116,7 +116,7 @@
 /// assert_eq!(request(0x101, 3), Err(OperationOutOfRange));
 /// ```
 pub const fn request(operation: u64, byte_count: u64) -> Result<u64, OperationOutOfRange> {
-    if operation > 0xff {
+    if operation > 0x1ff {
         return Err(OperationOutOfRange);
     }
     Ok(pack_request(operation as u8, byte_count))
